{"title":"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 21","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Richard_Rusczyk\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Richard Rusczyk</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>How many positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are there such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and the least common multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/3/2933f407c21ceb3f3ec8370f43cd5b939250ffca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> times the greatest common divisor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/2/c824ea490ff073932a1c17d8ce0b9a4215f90d95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10!$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/8/a28578f91569670ce8f5d1c27b4ea637a09a75a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n?$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/8/b18cb34f194027141cc51dbec9763bad5ebdbf95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 12 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 24 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } 36 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 48 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 72$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"398\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We set up the following equation as the problem states:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/1/e4182bee46f971119822c1847de1c6dd5e26a37f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{lcm}{(5!, n)} = 5\\text{gcd}{(10!, n)}.\\]\" width=\"191\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Breaking each number into its prime factorization, we see that the equation becomes\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/6/356f8d9a66c1cdd0ad995e7193a7bed51528f404.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{lcm}{(2^3\\cdot 3 \\cdot 5, n)} = 5\\text{gcd}{(2^8\\cdot 3^4 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7, n)}.\\]\" width=\"312\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>We can now determine the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. We know that its prime factors belong to the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/8/fb80775466cdd77221b3caa59054784159cdae44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{2, 3, 5, 7\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" />, as no factor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/2/c824ea490ff073932a1c17d8ce0b9a4215f90d95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10!$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c6878713578626763c38433b3f4c8c2205ad0c15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> in its prime factorization, nor anything greater. Next, we must find exactly how many different possibilities exist for each.\n</p><p>There can be anywhere between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s. However, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and we multiply the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/5/90532c5e1e39f45c92592c869e856d05ae869465.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{gcd}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, there can only be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />'s prime factorization. Finally, there can either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />'s.\n</p><p>Thus, we can multiply the total possibilities of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />'s factorization to determine the number of integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> which satisfy the equation, giving us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/e/b6e240d02c4a0312d73dbf806cee804ac2000595.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 \\times 4 \\times 1 \\times 2 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 48}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"193\" height=\"28\" />. ~ciceronii\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We do casework based on the values of \\( n \\) to uniquely determine the values of \\( \\text{lcm}(5!, n) \\). \n</p><p>Case 1: \\( n \\le 5! \\)\n</p><p>If \\( n \\le 5! \\), then \\( \\text{lcm}(n, 5!) = 120 \\). Then, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/7/05760fabd7fdb1a5e675f005c6dc6919f029cd3c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[120 = 5 \\gcd(10!, n)\\]\" width=\"144\" height=\"18\" />\nOr, \\( 24 = \\gcd(10!, n) \\). However, this cannot be possible, as \\( 10! \\) and \\( n \\) share a common \\( 5 \\). Then, the \\( \\gcd \\) must also be a multiple of \\( 5 \\), but \\( 24 \\) is not a multiple. Thus, the second case must be true.\n</p><p>Case 2: \\( n &gt; 5! \\).\n</p><p>If \\( n &gt; 5! \\), we have \\( \\text{lcm}(n, 5!) = n \\). Then, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/a/8caa27fd823452ab3333005888efe9b75b75776c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[n = 5 \\gcd(10!, n)\\]\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" />\nThe prime factorization of \\( 10! \\) is \\( 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7 \\). Then, the \\( \\gcd \\) is the highest power of the number of factors between \\( 10! \\) and \\( n \\). The prime factorization of \\( n \\) must have \\( 5^j \\). Then, we see that \\( j = 2 \\), because if \\( j = 1 \\) then we have that when we divide \\( 5 \\) on both sides, one side will contain no power of \\( 5 \\) and the other will have a power of \\( 5 \\). Thus, \\( n \\) has a prime factorization \\( 2^a 3^b 5^2 7^c \\). Then, we have \\( n &gt; 120 \\). To account for this, we divide by \\( 120 \\) on both sides to obtain a number with the prime factorization \\( 2^5 \\cdot 3^3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\). Thus, \\( n \\) is determined by the factors of this number (excluding \\( 5 \\), since \\( n \\) must have \\( 5 \\)). Then, the number of unique \\( n \\) is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/5/a756769e5fa852ea7288a33d85b08c7b60e1ce63.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(5+1)(3+1)(1+1) = 6\\times 4 \\times 2 = \\boxed{048}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"324\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p><p><br />\nThis solution incorrectly assumes  \\( \\text{lcm}(n, 5!) = 120  \\) and  \\( \\text{lcm}(n, 5!) = n \\). In case 1, \\( \\text{lcm}(n, 5!) = 120  \\) but this assumes \\( n \\) must be a factor of 5!, for example \\( n \\) = 25, 35, 45, 50, 55 <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/a/5caadd690c6520e910452fa650db87411e6e356f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$($\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"6\" height=\"18\" />and more<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/8/85852dfb3301b06f35017d1598f80915cce9a9ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"5\" height=\"18\" /> are all excluded. Case 2 claims that if \\(n &gt; 5!\\), then \\( \\text{lcm}(n, 5!) = n \\) . \\( \\text{lcm}(n, 5!) = n \\) is only true if 5! | \\( n \\). Merely being larger than 120 (n &gt; 120) does not force 120 to divide \\( n \\). For example, \\( n \\) = 125 satisfies \\( n \\) &gt; 5! and 5 | \\( n \\), but \\( \\text{lcm}(125, 120) = 3000 \\) <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/a/5caadd690c6520e910452fa650db87411e6e356f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$($\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"6\" height=\"18\" />which is not 125<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/8/85852dfb3301b06f35017d1598f80915cce9a9ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"5\" height=\"18\" />. This solution is not rigorous but it works by coincidence. ¯\\_(ツ)_/¯\n</p><p>-Akshaj S\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like the Solution 1, we start from the equation:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/1/e4182bee46f971119822c1847de1c6dd5e26a37f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{lcm}{(5!, n)} = 5\\text{gcd}{(10!, n)}.\\]\" width=\"191\" height=\"18\" />\nAssume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/e/98e5448715aa5a3999dbb65f064dcb7f2b452900.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{lcm}{(5!, n)}=k\\cdot5!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"133\" height=\"18\" />, with some integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. It follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/f/f8fd7e2826c68c32653fc3a0573d79395a0c310d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\cdot 4!=\\text{gcd}{(10!, n)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"141\" height=\"18\" />. It means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> has a divisor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/a/2da866442e69c040daf44260b4728d37e98de313.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4!$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> has a divisor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/3/2933f407c21ceb3f3ec8370f43cd5b939250ffca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/d/3edbb38985062ad29eefb20344d352b8a85f1291.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{lcm}{(5!, n)}=n=k\\cdot5!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"168\" height=\"18\" />. The equation can be changed as\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/1/d31c9697395029464e6406de5cf7d6476252298e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k\\cdot5!=5\\text{gcd}{(10!, k\\cdot5!)}\\]\" width=\"175\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/3/fc3a8c770bc89280e8eb9162077d861b996025b1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k=5\\text{gcd}{(6\\cdot7\\cdot8\\cdot9\\cdot10, k)}\\]\" width=\"205\" height=\"18\" />\nWe can see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> is also a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, with a form of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/f/73f5e0239d740ddf8df179f77b1d6d81f27c8669.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5\\cdot m$\" width=\"37\" height=\"12\" />. Substituting it in the above equation, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/9/f89ecf44bcf7f87a44bc62df275846bd1931d16f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[m=5\\text{gcd}{(6\\cdot7\\cdot8\\cdot9\\cdot2, m)}\\]\" width=\"208\" height=\"18\" />\nSimilarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> is a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, with a form of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/8/268cb55a7fc1750bae44c2712d8ff4283bb36aef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5\\cdot s$\" width=\"30\" height=\"12\" />. We have \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/a/7da21de3c788627db510b0d6e41ecd7f19478dc2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[s=\\text{gcd}{(6\\cdot7\\cdot8\\cdot9\\cdot2, 5\\cdot s)}=\\text{gcd}{(2^5\\cdot3^3\\cdot7, s)}\\]\" width=\"355\" height=\"19\" />\nThe equation holds, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> is a divisor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f8db5eb57499c0e8a8fd00ec9449c716aa13e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^5\\cdot3^3\\cdot7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"70\" height=\"17\" />, which has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/8/c4830c71e4efea7683c89d892a9401c67a6e29d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(5+1)(3+1)(1+1)=\\boxed{(\\textbf{D})48}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"246\" height=\"29\" /> divisors. ~Linty Huang.shen k.kai\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>As in the previous solutions, we start with\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/6/6068059d0e947abfe440e5b9b1ce3449076c1c0a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\text{lcm}(5!,n) = 5\\text{gcd}(10!,n)\\]\" width=\"186\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>From this we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/f/4ef58d0c9a5a08ab3783f96d180336d97a61b0a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{lcm}(5!,n) \\,|\\, 5\\text{gcd}(10!,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"173\" height=\"18\" /> , and in particular, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/1/65127add6157aaff46df40d6b2e1fe61ba262ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\,|\\, 5\\text{gcd}(10!,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"111\" height=\"18\" />. However, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/a/c8abfee781bcdd36648565b3765defcc30a8cbb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{gcd}(10!,n)\\, |\\, n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"103\" height=\"18\" />, so we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/f/aef2d8e01007e0c4b0c5ea0b76ac0b05a7058eb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{gcd}(10!,n) = n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"118\" height=\"20\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/9/8e918f9bd38398cb3b0c8f0cf78f7daa64f6f6b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{gcd}(10!,n) = n/5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"134\" height=\"18\" />. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/f/aef2d8e01007e0c4b0c5ea0b76ac0b05a7058eb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{gcd}(10!,n) = n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"118\" height=\"20\" />, then we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/b/b1bea33ad5ef50915737f06fab4c86110b69c5c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{lcm}(5!,n) = 5n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"117\" height=\"18\" />; because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/3/1f37c03e74c30f731e259959437671f820b1a60e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5\\, |\\, 5!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" />, this implies that 5 does not divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, so we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/9/8e918f9bd38398cb3b0c8f0cf78f7daa64f6f6b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{gcd}(10!,n) = n/5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"134\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Now we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/d/98d2f2b1aeb2172271cd88ccc8aa92add5f8ad72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{lcm}(5!,n) = n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />, implying that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/2/e52109b0a0028baddead9c43763a7d84b1ebbba5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5!\\, |\\, n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/c/08c78d2a655de854782c6c1376ba40cf1f4aaa8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n/5\\, |\\, 10!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />. Writing out prime factorizations, this gives us\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/e/b0eb55065ccc5991ef28ad44bf5c18ee16b4e115.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2^3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\,|\\, n\\]\" width=\"82\" height=\"19\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/7/ca76c1325659c16b4301a055f60ee9fdfa8ad551.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[n \\,|\\, 2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7\\]\" width=\"119\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> can have 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, or 8 factors of 2; 1, 2, 3, or 4 factors of two; and 0 or 1 factors of 7. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/6/106d87c7c9bdc9f1f7da940643f198fdbbf0ac8d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{gcd}(2^8 \\cdot 3^4 \\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 7,n) = n/5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"211\" height=\"21\" /> implies that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> has 2 factors of 5. Thus, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/4/5d4f00e1e9a16789f1496479014383e1832a17c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 2 = 48$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"95\" height=\"13\" /> possible choices for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, and our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/d/2ed4dfd7393ac0f517b99c3471d1f5f714ef3275.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) 48}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"66\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>-gumbymoo\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Richard_Rusczyk\">Video Solution by Richard Rusczyk</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Richard Rusczyk\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8S85536jpYw&amp;list=PLyhPcpM8aMvJvwA2kypmfdtlxH90ShZCc&amp;index=1&amp;t=25s\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8S85536jpYw&amp;list=PLyhPcpM8aMvJvwA2kypmfdtlxH90ShZCc&amp;index=1&amp;t=25s</a>\n- AMBRIGGS\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/CWZkTCNu42o?t=846\">https://youtu.be/CWZkTCNu42o?t=846</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A\">2020 AMC 12A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_20\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 20\">Problem 20</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 22\">Problem 22</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_3\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_4\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_5\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_7\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_8\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_11\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 15\">15</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_16\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 16\">16</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 17\">17</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_18\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 18\">18</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_19\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 19\">19</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_20\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 20\">20</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">21</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 22\">22</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_23\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 23\">23</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_24\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 24\">24</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2020_AMC_12A_Problems/Problem_25\" title=\"2020 AMC 12A Problems/Problem 25\">25</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_12_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AMC 12 Problems and Solutions\"> All AMC 12 Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017034648\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.051 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.074 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 544/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 2298/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 158/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 15798/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   13.736      1 -total\n 60.08%    8.253      1 Template:AMC12_box\n 38.31%    5.262      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:18139:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017034648 and revision id 289040. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}